use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.Array
use opensp.StringC.StringC
use dazzle.TestScratch.TestScratch
use opensp.Location.Location
use opensp.Location.InputSource
use opensp.Location.Origin
use opensp.Location.TextChunk
use opensp.ParserState.ParserState
use opensp.Parser.Parser
use opensp.Event.Event
use opensp.ElementType.ElementType
use opensp.AttributeList.AttributeList
use opensp.Attribute.Attribute
use opensp.Attribute.AttributeValue
use opensp.Attribute.AttributeSemantics
use opensp.Attribute.Text
use opensp.Entity.Entity
use opensp.Entity.InternalEntity
use opensp.Notation.Notation
use opensp.ExternalId.ExternalId
use opensp.Dtd.Dtd
use opensp.ArcEngine.ArcEngine
use dazzle.Interpreter.Interpreter
use dazzle.InterpreterMessages.InterpreterMessages
use dazzle.ELObj.ELObj
use dazzle.ELObj.OutputCharStream
use dazzle.SchemeParser.SchemeParser
use scaly.os.File

; DssslSpecEventHandler — assembles a DSSSL style specification from a parsed
; `.dsl` document (style/DssslSpecEventHandler.cxx / .cs). A `.dsl` is an SGML
; document conforming to the DSSSL style-sheet DTD; parsing it produces the
; STYLE-SHEET / STYLE-SPECIFICATION / STYLE-SPECIFICATION-BODY element events
; with the Scheme source arriving as character data (external `.scm` text
; entities + CDATA marked sections are expanded inline by the parser into DATA
; events). This handler walks those events, assembles the Part/Doc structure,
; extracts each part's body text, and resolves the USE closure — the ordered
; list of parts the interpreter then reads with the SchemeParser.
;
; SEAM (the ArcEngine/Rast precedent): a POST-PARSE pass over `ps.events`
; (opensp.Parser.parse_simple produced them), not a live EventHandler. The C++
; loadDoc routes the parse through ArcEngine to SELECT the DSSSL architecture
; (arcEventHandler matches the arch's notation public id); the C# mirror
; DIVERGES and parses directly ("ArcEngine architecture processing is
; incomplete").
;
; ★Since 2026-08-09 the port does route it (route_through_arch): the walk below
; consumes the DSSSL architecture's GENERATED stream, which is loadDoc's
; `ArcEngine::parseAll`. Two halves make that possible, and both are in opensp:
; the validating half of the engine (ArcEngine.scaly's STATUS note) and
; mungeMetaDtd, without which the arch stream carries no ENTITY semantics — and
; `document`/`content` below are exactly ENTITY-declared attributes, so a
; routing without it would have cut the 37-document chain of a DocBook style
; sheet off in silence.
;
; The architecture GATE stays where it was and keeps its own job:
; check_dsssl_arch asks opensp whether the spec declares the DSSSL architecture
; as a BASE architecture (`IS10744 ArcBase` PI + a notation with the arch public
; id, the conjunction that sets the reference's gotArc_) and reports specNotArc
; when it does not, processing on with no rules. It must run BEFORE the routing,
; because it reads the PI that the routing replaces.
;
; Inc-2 scope: single-document assembly with USE-within-document part chaining
; and inline/gathered bodies (ImmediateBodyElement). The cross-file
; EXTERNAL-SPECIFICATION path (a `document` entity referencing another `.dsl`,
; resolved by a NESTED parse) is ported structurally; its resolve re-parses
; through parse_simple when a system id is present.

function getenv(name: pointer[const_char]) returns pointer[const_char] extern
procedure scaly_eputs(s: pointer[const_char]) extern
procedure scaly_eputi(value: i64) extern
procedure scaly_eputnl() extern

; --- the per-document parse ARENA ------------------------------------------
;
; A style sheet is not one SGML document but 38 (`docbook.dsl` declares 37
; <external-specification>, each loaded by its own parse), and every one of
; those parses used to live to program end on the run region — 234 of 270 MB
; requested, measured by tests/dazzle/startbytes. What survives a spec parse is
; the assembled Part/Doc structure and, per body element, the gathered text plus
; its chunk table; all of that is on the handler's own host, and since the
; pinning step (Origin.pin, StringC copies in declaration_start) NOTHING in it
; points into the parse any more. So each document's parse gets an exclusive
; page chain that falls when its walk is done.
;
; ★The escape hatch is not comfort. Condition (b) below is an audit of the
; structures the walk keeps, and an audit is exactly the kind of claim that is
; invisible when it is wrong (the frame mark's store sweep, 2026-08-06) —
; `DZ_SPEC_ARENA=0` restores the old lifetime, and tests/dazzle/specarena
; checks BOTH paths.
;
; NOT freed, two conditions:
;   (a) DZ_SPEC_ARENA=0.
;   (b) the document produced a BodyElement.Entity (a CONTENT= entity body).
;       That arm keeps a pointer[Entity] INTO the parse, and make_input_source
;       dereferences it after every parse is over. Pinning it would mean a deep
;       copy of an Entity, and no corpus style sheet uses the form — an
;       untested pin is worse than a held arena, and the hold costs nothing
;       measurable because it never fires.

; DssslSpecEventHandler::DeclarationType
define DECL_FEATURES: int 0
define DECL_BASESET_ENCODING: int 1
define DECL_LITERAL_DESCRIBED_CHAR: int 2
define DECL_ADD_NAME_CHARS: int 3
define DECL_ADD_SEPARATOR_CHARS: int 4
define DECL_STANDARD_CHARS: int 5
define DECL_OTHER_CHARS: int 6
define DECL_COMBINE_CHAR: int 7
define DECL_MAP_SDATA_ENTITY: int 8
define DECL_CHAR_REPERTOIRE: int 9
define DECL_SGML_GROVE_PLAN: int 10

; opensp Event.event_type discriminants (module-level consts in opensp.Event
; are not cross-package-visible; mirrored here — the values are the stable
; Event ABI: EVK_START=0, EVK_END=1, EVK_DATA=2).
define EVK_START: int 0
define EVK_END: int 1
define EVK_DATA: int 2
define EVK_END_PROLOG: int 8

; --- the assembled structures (Link subclasses in the reference) -----------

; A DSSSL declaration element's gathered content (features, char-repertoire,
; standard-chars, …). name/text/modadd/desc are the attribute-sourced fields.
; ★`chunks` is the declaration's own location table, kept for the same
; reason `ImmediateBody` keeps one: the four declaration parsers DO
; report (badDeclaration, badCharName, duplicateCharName), and without
; the table a diagnostic in an entity-expanded declaration anchors
; nowhere. StyleEngine.cxx builds the same thing —
; `TextInputSourceOrigin(content_)`.
define DeclarationElement (decl_type: int, var content: StringC, var chunks: ref[Array[TextChunk]]?, var name: StringC, var text: StringC, var modadd: StringC, var desc: StringC)
{
    function make(host: ref[Page], decl_type: int) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[DeclarationElement] linked





    function get_type(this: DeclarationElement) @ret_param_0 true returns int linked


    function get_content(this: DeclarationElement) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns StringC linked


    function get_name(this: DeclarationElement) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns StringC linked


    function get_text(this: DeclarationElement) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns StringC linked

}

; A body element: either immediate gathered text or an entity reference
; (STYLE-SPECIFICATION-BODY with a CONTENT= entity attribute). The immediate
; arm carries the per-chunk source-location table (the reference Text object)
; so a diagnostic in the gathered Scheme resolves to the original source —
; a chunk gathered from an expanded `&file.scm;` reference resolves INTO that
; file (TextInputSourceOrigin::defLocation → Text::charLocation).
define ImmediateBody (text: StringC, chunks: ref[Array[TextChunk]]?)
define EntityBody (entity: ref[Entity]?)

define BodyElement union
(
    Immediate: ImmediateBody
    Entity: EntityBody
)
{
    function make_immediate(host: ref[Page], text: StringC, chunks: ref[Array[TextChunk]]) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[BodyElement] linked





    function make_entity(host: ref[Page], entity: ref[Entity]) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[BodyElement] linked




}

; A concrete specification part: its declarations, body elements, and the
; headers of the parts it USEs.
define Part
(
    declarations: ref[Array[ref[DeclarationElement]?]]
    body_elements: ref[Array[ref[BodyElement]?]]
    use_headers: ref[Array[ref[PartHeader]?]]
    var mark: bool
    doc: ref[Doc]?
)
{
    function make(host: ref[Page], doc: ref[Doc]?) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[Part] linked





    procedure add_use(mutable this: ref[Part], header: ref[PartHeader]) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true linked


    procedure append_body(mutable this: ref[Part], b: ref[BodyElement]) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true linked


    procedure append_decl(mutable this: ref[Part], d: ref[DeclarationElement]?) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true linked


    function get_body_elements(this: Part) @ret_param_0 true returns ref[Array[ref[BodyElement]?]] linked


    ; the document this part came from — StyleEngine::parseSpec reads its
    ; declarations before the part's own.
    function get_doc(this: Part) @ret_param_0 true returns ref[Doc]? linked


    ; StyleEngine::parseSpec walks the DOC's declarations and then the PART's
    ; (the `local` do-while), so both lists need an accessor.
    function get_declarations(this: Part) @ret_param_0 true returns ref[Array[ref[DeclarationElement]?]] linked


    function get_use_headers(this: Part) @ret_param_0 true returns ref[Array[ref[PartHeader]?]] linked


    ; setMark(b): return the previous mark, set the new one (use-loop guard).
    procedure set_mark(mutable this: ref[Part], b: bool) @ret_param_0 true returns bool linked





}

; SpecPart — how a PartHeader resolves to a concrete Part.
define ExternalPartRef (header: ref[PartHeader])
define ExternalFirstPartRef (doc: ref[Doc])
define ConcretePartRef (part: ref[Part])

define SpecPart union
(
    External: ExternalPartRef
    ExternalFirst: ExternalFirstPartRef
    Concrete: ConcretePartRef
)

; A reference to a specification part by ID within a document.
define PartHeader
(
    doc: ref[Doc]
    id: StringC
    var ref_loc: Location
    var spec_part: ref[SpecPart]?
)
{
    function make(host: ref[Page], doc: ref[Doc], id: StringC) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[PartHeader] linked





    function get_id(this: PartHeader) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns StringC linked


    ; setRefLoc — first reference wins (the reference guards on a null origin).
    ; The location is PINNED (Origin.pin): a header outlives the parse of the
    ; document that referenced it, and missingPart renders this anchor later,
    ; from resolve_parts.
    procedure set_ref_loc(mutable this: ref[PartHeader], host: ref[Page], mutable loc: Location) linked





    procedure set_concrete(mutable this: ref[PartHeader], host: ref[Page], part: ref[Part]) linked






    procedure set_external(mutable this: ref[PartHeader], host: ref[Page], header: ref[PartHeader]) linked






    procedure set_external_first(mutable this: ref[PartHeader], host: ref[Page], doc: ref[Doc]) linked






    ; PartHeader::resolve — force the owning doc loaded, then resolve the part.
    procedure resolve(mutable this: ref[PartHeader], mutable eh: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler]) returns ref[Part]? io reads msg_prog linked










}

; A source document (the main `.dsl` or a referenced external spec file).
define Doc
(
    var loaded: bool
    sysid: StringC
    declarations: ref[Array[ref[DeclarationElement]?]]
    headers: ref[Array[ref[PartHeader]?]]
    var loc: Location
)
{
    function make(host: ref[Page], sysid: StringC) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[Doc] linked





    function get_declarations(this: Doc) @ret_param_0 true returns ref[Array[ref[DeclarationElement]?]] linked


    function get_sysid(this: Doc) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns StringC linked


    ; refPart(id): find or create the header for a part ID in this doc.
    procedure ref_part(mutable this: ref[Doc], host: ref[Page], id: StringC) returns ref[PartHeader] linked
















    procedure append_decl(mutable this: ref[Doc], d: ref[DeclarationElement]?) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true linked


    ; the doc's own location (its end-prolog event; anchors noParts) — PINNED
    ; for the same reason as PartHeader.ref_loc: resolve_first_part renders it
    ; after the walk that produced it.
    procedure set_location(mutable this: ref[Doc], host: ref[Page], mutable loc: Location) linked


    ; resolveFirstPart: the FIRST-created header's part — the reference walks
    ; its headers_ IList to the last iteration position, and IList::insert
    ; PREPENDS, so that is the EARLIEST refPart (the document's first
    ; style-specification in practice; a USE ref can create headers later).
    procedure resolve_first_part(mutable this: ref[Doc], mutable eh: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler]) returns ref[Part]? io reads msg_prog linked

















    ; Doc::load — parse the referenced spec file (sysid) and assemble it.
    ; The main doc (empty sysid) is already assembled by the driver's walk;
    ; this fires only for an external-spec document with its own file.
    procedure load(mutable this: ref[Doc], mutable eh: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler]) io reads APP_INPUT_CS linked








}

; --- the handler -----------------------------------------------------------
define DssslSpecEventHandler
(
    host: ref[Page]
    interp: ref[Interpreter]
    var got_arc: bool
    var gathering_body: bool
    var current_part: ref[Part]?
    current_body: ref[Array[u32]]
    ; the gathered text's per-chunk source locations (the reference gathers
    ; into a Text, which records a chunk per addChars) — cleared with
    ; current_body, snapshotted into the ImmediateBody.
    current_chunks: ref[Array[TextChunk]]
    var current_doc: ref[Doc]?
    var current_decl: ref[DeclarationElement]?
    ; condition (b) of the per-document arena: this document's walk kept a
    ; pointer INTO its parse. Saved/restored around every walk like current_doc,
    ; and read directly by load_stylesheet after load() returns — the accessor
    ; that used to sit beside it was SHADOWED by this field at every call site
    ; (a field wins over a same-named method, silently), so it was never the
    ; thing that ran.
    var keeps_parse: bool
    docs: ref[Array[ref[Doc]?]]
    ; parse parameters for loading EXTERNAL-SPECIFICATION documents (the
    ; reference derives a child SgmlParser from the main parser's params;
    ; we re-parse with the same program name and DSSSL catalog).
    prog_name: String
    catalog_path: String
    ; chunk-table coalescing (Text::addChars, see the walk's data arm): the
    ; chunks kept and the characters gathered over this handler's walks. The
    ; mechanism is INVISIBLE in the output — one chunk per character resolves
    ; every offset to the same location — so the self-test reads the ratio.
    var chunks_kept: i64
    var chunk_chars: i64
    ; the per-document arenas this handler released and held (condition (b)) —
    ; what `--spec-arena-stats` prints, the gate's proof that the arena engages
    var arenas_released: i64
    var arenas_held: i64
)
{
    function make(host: ref[Page], interp: ref[Interpreter], prog_name: String, catalog_path: String) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[DssslSpecEventHandler] linked





    ; DZ_SPEC_ARENA — see the arena comment at the top of this file.
    ; read per spec DOCUMENT — a style sheet has a few dozen — so there is no
    ; state worth caching
    procedure spec_arena_on() @ret_nonlocal true returns bool io linked








    ; the parse host for ONE spec document: an exclusive page chain off `host`
    ; when the arena is on, otherwise `host` itself (the historical lifetime).
    procedure spec_arena(host: ref[Page]) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[Page] io linked






    ; …and its release. `keep` is condition (b), decided by the walk.
    procedure release_spec_arena(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], host: ref[Page], arena: ref[Page], keep: bool) linked












    ; `--spec-arena-stats`: the gate's proof that the arena and the chunk
    ; coalescing engaged — both are invisible in the output
    procedure report_spec_arena(this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler]) @ret_nonlocal true io linked














    ; findDoc(sysid): the doc registry (one Doc per system id; empty = main).
    procedure find_doc(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], sysid: StringC) @ret_param_0 true returns ref[Doc] linked
















    ; SpecPart::resolve dispatch.
    procedure resolve_spec_part(mutable sp: ref[SpecPart]?, mutable eh: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler]) returns ref[Part]? io reads msg_prog linked















    ; check_dsssl_arch — loadDoc's gotArc_ gate: the document must declare the
    ; DSSSL architecture as a BASE architecture, i.e. carry an `IS10744 ArcBase`
    ; PI naming an architecture whose notation in the DTD has the DSSSL arch
    ; public id. That conjunction (ArcBase PI + lookupNotation + the director's
    ; pubid test) lives in opensp, where the ArcEngine already parses the PI form.
    ;
    ; ★It used to test the NOTATION alone, which is strictly weaker: a spec whose
    ; internal subset declares the DSSSL notation but no ArcBase PI passed here
    ; while both reference binaries report specNotArc and then process with NO
    ; rules (tests/dazzle/cli/notarc.dsl pins the difference).
    procedure check_dsssl_arch(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], ps: ref[ParserState]) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


    ; --- attribute helpers -------------------------------------------------
    function attribute_string(ev: ref[Event], name: Slice[char]) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns StringC linked
















    ; attributeText — the attribute's value Text (per-char source locations).
    function attribute_text(ev: ref[Event], name: Slice[char]) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[Text]? linked













    ; Text::charLocation(i) — the recorded per-char location (one past the
    ; last for i == size, like the reference).
    function text_char_loc(t: ref[Text], i: size_t) @page true @direct true returns Location linked













    function attribute_entity(ev: ref[Event], name: Slice[char]) @page true @direct true returns ref[Entity]? linked


















    ; --- element handlers (the mapping table's targets) --------------------
    procedure external_specification_start(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], mutable ev: ref[Event]) linked



































    procedure style_specification_start(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], mutable ev: ref[Event]?) linked






































    procedure style_specification_end(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler]) linked


    procedure style_specification_body_start(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], ev: ref[Event]?) linked

























    procedure style_specification_body_end(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler]) linked









































    procedure declaration_start(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], ev: ref[Event]?, decl_type: int) linked




























    procedure declaration_end(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler]) linked






























    ; the decl-type of a declaration GI (the reference's declarationStart switch).
    function decl_type_of(gi: StringC) returns int linked


























    function is_declaration_gi(gi: StringC) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


    ; --- the event walk (mapping-table dispatch) ---------------------------
    procedure walk(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], mutable ps: ref[ParserState]) linked

















































































































    ; load an external spec document by re-parsing its file, then walk it
    ; (reference Doc::load → loadDoc: a child SgmlParser over the sysid, the
    ; DSSSL-architecture gate per document, currentDoc_ rebound for the walk).
    procedure load_external_doc(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], doc: ref[Doc]) @ret_nonlocal true io reads APP_INPUT_CS linked


























































    ; loadDoc's `ArcEngine::parseAll` (DssslSpecEventHandler.cxx:88): replace the
    ; base event stream with the DSSSL architecture's generated one, so the walk
    ; below sees the ARCHITECTURAL forms. Called only once the gate has already
    ; said the architecture is declared — the gate reads the ArcBase PI out of
    ; the base events, which this call removes, so the ORDER is load-bearing.
    ;
    ; The host is the PARSE's own region, not the handler's: the generated stream
    ; and the meta-DTD must live exactly as long as the base parse they came from
    ; (the reference allocates them in the ArcEngineImpl's Allocator, same
    ; lifetime), and that is also what makes the per-document arena above still
    ; correct — condition (b) holds the arena for a CONTENT= entity, and under
    ; routing that entity is the meta-DTD's COPY, which sits on the same arena.
    procedure route_through_arch(mutable ps: ref[ParserState]) @ret_nonlocal true io reads STDERR_ENC linked








    ; --- top-level entry ---------------------------------------------------
    ; load(): assemble the parts of the main document from ps.events. Returns
    ; the ordered part list (the resolved part + its USE closure).
    ;
    ; A document that does not declare the DSSSL architecture as a base
    ; architecture is NOT fatal: loadDoc reports specNotArc and RETURNS, and
    ; load() then runs its normal tail over a doc that was never walked — so the
    ; part list comes back EMPTY and the engine processes with no construction
    ; rules (default processing). Nothing is skipped besides the walk: an
    ; explicit `-d sysid#ID` still creates its header and still reports the
    ; missing-ID error, exactly like the reference. resolve_first_part stays
    ; silent because the doc's location is only set by the (never delivered)
    ; end-prolog event.
    procedure load(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], mutable ps: ref[ParserState], mutable id: StringC) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[Array[ref[Part]?]]? io reads msg_prog linked


























    ; resolveParts: collect the part and, transitively, its USE closure
    ; (mark guards the use-loop).
    procedure resolve_parts(mutable this: ref[DssslSpecEventHandler], mutable part: ref[Part]?, mutable parts: ref[Array[ref[Part]?]]) @page true @direct true io reads msg_prog linked






















    ; makeInputSource (BodyElement::makeInputSource): ONE InputSource per body
    ; element, each read by its own SchemeParser (StyleEngine::parseSpec). An
    ; immediate body reads the gathered text through a text origin whose chunk
    ; table resolves a diagnostic back into the original source — a chunk
    ; gathered from an expanded `&file.scm;` reference anchors INSIDE that
    ; file. A CONTENT= internal entity reads its replacement text under an
    ; entity origin with no reference location (EntityOrigin::make(entity,
    ; Location()) — renders "(invalid location)", like the reference). The
    ; CONTENT= EXTERNAL entity fresh-open (entityManager().open) is not
    ; wired yet — no corpus stylesheet uses it; returns null (skipped).
    ; DeclarationElement::makeInputSource (DssslSpecEventHandler.cxx:523) —
    ; a TextInputSourceOrigin over the gathered content, i.e. exactly the
    ; Immediate body arm below with the declaration's own chunk table.
    ; StyleEngine::parseSpec's DECLARATION phase (StyleEngine.cxx:40-80) — the
    ; part of the spec that runs BEFORE any Scheme body, and which this port
    ; gathered but never executed until 2026-08-08 (found by the dazzle-side
    ; message audit: `Interpreter.set_char_repertoire` and the four
    ; `SchemeParser.parse_*` declaration readers were fully ported and had ZERO
    ; callers).
    ;
    ; Three shapes of the reference are load-bearing here:
    ;
    ;  * TWO PHASES over the whole part list. `char-repertoire` and
    ;    `standard-chars` run in phase 0, everything else in phase 1 — a
    ;    `map-sdata-entity` in the FIRST part may name a character that the LAST
    ;    part's `standard-chars` defines, so the character tables must be complete
    ;    before anything reads them.
    ;  * per part, the DOC's declarations first, then the PART's own (the
    ;    reference's `local` do-while).
    ;  * `dEndPart` after each part, in BOTH phases — the declaration phase has
    ;    its own part counter, and it is what decides whether a redefinition
    ;    overwrites, is ignored, or is a duplicate diagnostic.
    procedure parse_declarations(host: ref[Page], mutable interp: ref[Interpreter], parts: ref[Array[ref[Part]?]]) io reads msg_prog linked














































    ; the switch of StyleEngine::parseSpec. ★Every arm gets its OWN SchemeParser
    ; over the declaration's own input source, exactly as the reference does —
    ; the reader state (token, current entity) must not leak between declarations.
    procedure run_declaration(host: ref[Page], mutable interp: ref[Interpreter], mutable d: ref[DeclarationElement], ty: int) io reads msg_prog linked


















































    function make_decl_input_source(host: ref[Page], d: ref[DeclarationElement]) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[InputSource]? linked








    function make_input_source(host: ref[Page], be: ref[BodyElement]?) @ret_explicit_0 true returns ref[InputSource]? linked































    ; body_text(part): concatenate the part's immediate body elements into one
    ; StringC — the Scheme source the SchemeParser reads. (Entity body elements
    ; are resolved by the interpreter at read time; Inc 2 gathers immediate.)
    function body_text(host: ref[Page], part: ref[Part]) @page true @direct true @ret_explicit_0 true returns StringC linked



























    ; --- self-test ---------------------------------------------------------
    ; Parse a self-contained `.dsl` (inline DTD subset declaring the style-sheet
    ; forms + the DSSSL arch notation for the gate), assemble the part, extract
    ; the body, and dump its datums through the SchemeParser — the Inc-2 seam
    ; end to end: opensp parse → part assembly → Scheme reader.
    procedure test() @ret_nonlocal true returns int io reads APP_INPUT_CS linked




































































































}
